    29	
    30	## 1. Standalone shape
    31	
    32	| Field | |
    33	|---|---|
    34	| **What** | CLI-first product (`npx @boring/agent`); same product shape as Claude Code. |
    35	| **Why** | Developers need a zero-config entry point. CLI is the lowest-friction distribution for Node tooling. |
    36	| **Rationale** | Library-first would require users to wire up a server. CLI ships a working experience out of the box and can still be imported as a library. |
    37	| **Re-evaluate when** | Library usage grows beyond CLI usage. |
    38	
    39	## 2. Chat UI
    40	
    41	| Field | |
    42	|---|---|
    43	| **What** | Vercel ai-elements (copied into repo) + `@ai-sdk/react useChat`. |
    44	| **Why** | ai-elements provides battle-tested chat primitives; copying avoids version coupling. |
    45	| **Rationale** | Copying (not depending) lets us diverge without waiting for upstream releases. `useChat` is the standard React hook for AI SDK streams. |
    46	| **Re-evaluate when** | ai-elements API stabilizes or a maintained fork emerges. |
    47	
    48	## 3. Wire protocol
    49	
    50	| Field | |
    51	|---|---|
    52	| **What** | AI SDK UIMessage stream end-to-end. |
    53	| **Why** | Single message format from harness through SSE to React avoids translation layers. |
    54	| **Rationale** | UIMessage is already what `useChat` expects. Any custom format would require a mapping step on both server and client. |
    55	| **Re-evaluate when** | AI SDK introduces breaking changes to the UIMessage format. |
    56	
    57	## 4. v1 harness
    58	
    59	| Field | |
    60	|---|---|
    61	| **What** | `@mariozechner/pi-coding-agent` as the v1 agent harness. |
    62	| **Why** | pi-coding-agent provides a working agent loop with tool execution, session management, and streaming. |
    63	| **Rationale** | Building an agent loop from scratch would delay v1 by weeks. pi-coding-agent is proven and actively maintained. |
    64	| **Re-evaluate when** | pi stability regresses OR ai-sdk harness needs arrive (boring-macro migration). |
    65	
    66	## 5. Harness interface
    67	
    68	| Field | |
    69	|---|---|
    70	| **What** | Generic `AgentHarness` interface with `placement: server \| browser`. Future browser-agent is a sibling adapter, not a migration. |
    71	| **Why** | Decouples the HTTP layer from the agent implementation. |
    72	| **Rationale** | A generic interface lets us swap pi-coding-agent for AI SDK or a browser-local agent without touching routes. The `placement` discriminator makes the constraint explicit. |
    73	| **Re-evaluate when** | A browser-agent harness is implemented and the interface needs revision. |
    74	
    75	## 6. Tool catalog
    76	
    77	| Field | |
    78	|---|---|
    79	| **What** | 4 base tools (bash, read, write, edit) + conditional `execute_isolated_code` (sandbox capability-gated). |
    80	| **Why** | Minimal baseline that covers core coding tasks. |
    81	| **Rationale** | More tools increase LLM context cost and confuse the model. Start with the essentials; richer sandboxes auto-lift the catalog. |
    82	| **Re-evaluate when** | User feedback shows missing tools block common workflows. |
    83	
    84	## 7a. Workspace (local)
    85	
    86	| Field | |
    87	|---|---|
    88	| **What** | `NodeWorkspace` with ported `validatePath` / `assertRealPathWithinWorkspace`. |
    89	| **Why** | Local file access with path-traversal protection. |
    90	| **Rationale** | Reusing validated path logic from prior work avoids re-introducing security bugs. |
    91	| **Re-evaluate when** | Node.js gains native workspace sandboxing APIs. |
    92	
    93	## 7b. Workspace (remote)
    94	
    95	| Field | |
    96	|---|---|
    97	| **What** | `VercelSandboxWorkspace` delegates to `sandbox.fs.*` + `sandbox.writeFiles`. |
    98	| **Why** | Remote execution requires delegating FS operations to the sandbox runtime. |
    99	| **Rationale** | Vercel's sandbox API provides a secure, ephemeral filesystem. Wrapping it behind the Workspace interface keeps the rest of the stack unaware of the execution context. |
   100	| **Re-evaluate when** | Alternative remote sandbox providers are needed. |
   101	
   102	## 7c. Sandbox (local)
   103	
   104	| Field | |
   105	|---|---|
   106	| **What** | `BwrapSandbox` (`capabilities: ['exec']`) pairs with NodeWorkspace. |
   107	| **Why** | Local code execution needs isolation from the host. |
   108	| **Rationale** | bubblewrap (bwrap) provides Linux namespace isolation without root. Capability-gated so the sandbox only exposes what the tool needs. |
   109	| **Re-evaluate when** | Non-Linux local execution is required (macOS, Windows). |
   110	
   111	## 7d. Sandbox (remote)
   112	
   113	| Field | |
   114	|---|---|
   115	| **What** | `VercelSandboxExec` (`capabilities: ['exec']`) pairs with VercelSandboxWorkspace. |
   116	| **Why** | Remote code execution via Vercel's sandbox runtime. |
   117	| **Rationale** | Pairs naturally with VercelSandboxWorkspace. Same capability interface as BwrapSandbox. |
   118	| **Re-evaluate when** | Alternative remote execution environments are needed. |
   119	
   120	## 7e. Pairing invariant
   121	
   122	| Field | |
   123	|---|---|
   124	| **What** | Workspace + Sandbox MUST target the same execution context. Enforced at adapter construction; no mixed pairings. The public workspace namespace must stay coherent across file tree root, shell cwd, model-visible cwd, and `BORING_AGENT_WORKSPACE_ROOT`. |
   125	| **Why** | A NodeWorkspace with a VercelSandboxExec would read local files but execute remotely -- silently broken. Leaking adapter-private roots into model-visible paths creates the same class of split-brain bug. |
   126	| **Rationale** | Compile-time/construction-time enforcement is cheaper than debugging subtle runtime mismatches. One public namespace keeps prompts, tools, and shell observations talking about the same place. |
   127	| **Re-evaluate when** | A legitimate cross-context pairing emerges. |
   128	
   129	> **Supersession note (#391 v2):** This pairing invariant remains binding for
   130	> any boring-bash-active runtime where a filesystem/exec environment is
   131	> attached. Pure/headless agents (`runtime: 'none'`) run without `Workspace`,
   132	> `Sandbox`, cwd, file routes, or bash tools, so there is no Workspace+Sandbox
   133	> pair to construct. When boring-bash is present, file tree, search/watch, bash,
   134	> git/status, and model-visible cwd must still share one source of truth.
   135	
   136	## 7f. Mode selection
   137	
   138	| Field | |
   139	|---|---|
   140	| **What** | `mode = "direct" \| "local" \| "vercel-sandbox"` in config. Env override via `BORING_AGENT_MODE`. |
   141	| **Why** | Each mode selects a Workspace + Sandbox pairing. |
   142	| **Rationale** | Three named modes cover all current deployment targets. Env override supports CI and container deploys. |
   143	| **Re-evaluate when** | A fourth execution context is needed. Note: non-builtin modes are already supported without core edits — pass a custom `runtimeModeAdapter` to `createAgentApp`/`registerAgentRoutes` (see `packages/agent/docs/runtime.md`). |
   144	
   145	## 8. Plugins
   146	
   147	| Field | |
   148	|---|---|
   149	| **What** | Coexist via pi extensions in direct/local modes only. Remote mode skips extension load. |
   150	| **Why** | Plugins are Node-native (require filesystem, child_process). Remote sandboxes can't load them. |
   151	| **Rationale** | Restricting to direct/local avoids silent failures in remote mode. Pi's extension system handles discovery and lifecycle. |
   152	| **Re-evaluate when** | A WASM-based plugin format enables remote extension loading. |
   153	
   154	## 9. Sessions
   155	
   156	| Field | |
   157	|---|---|
   158	| **What** | `SessionStore` interface + `PiSessionStore` (JSONL) in v1. |
   159	| **Why** | Conversation persistence across page reloads and server restarts. |
   160	| **Rationale** | JSONL is append-only, human-readable, and trivial to implement. The platform-agnostic interface allows future SQLite or IndexedDB backends. |
   161	| **Re-evaluate when** | Session data grows large enough to need indexed queries. |
   162	
   163	## 10. API key
   164	
   165	| Field | |
   166	|---|---|
   167	| **What** | `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` env var only. `VERCEL_OIDC_TOKEN` in remote mode. 12-factor. |
   168	| **Why** | No config files, no UI for secrets. |
   169	| **Rationale** | Env vars are the standard for secrets in server-side apps. Avoids accidentally committing keys. OIDC for remote mode enables keyless auth. |
   170	| **Re-evaluate when** | Multi-provider key management is needed. |
